WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  yemeni soldiers pushed back  houthi advances on two fronts north of  the city of madrid  battles like this one have intensified  since the start of the month  the houthis have been wrestling to take  the last saudi-backed government  stronghold  in northern yemen for over a year the  violence has resulted in dozens of  deaths  and in a tweet u.n humanitarian chief  mark lukakus warned  the assault would put two million lives  at risk  and could cause unimaginable  humanitarian consequences  the us is calling for the houthis to  halt their advance  you know our hope is that a combined  effort bringing in  certain partners at certain times packed  with a strong  very strong american position will um  will essentially shake up the  architecture and put us in a much better  place  to push for that negotiated settlement  which i think we all agree is the only  way forward  on tuesday the u.s lifted the previous  administration's designation  of the houthis as a terrorist  organization  and in a further departure president  biden ended u.s support  for the saudi-led military offensive in  yemen  for their part houthis accused the  yemeni government  of being the aggressors i don't see tim  lenderking at this point  approaching the houthis directly but  he probably should i understand the  significance of marib for them  they may gain a few kilometers they may  even gain the city  but they lose the peace and eventually  they will only prolong the war if they  persist  some analysts say houthis are using the  fight in marrab  as leverage in any future negotiations  but advancements have been slow with  saudi arabia intensifying airstrikes  rights groups say 650 families have been  displaced  and many more could follow but the u.s  administration  hopes it can prevent further bloodshed  and help bring the six-year war  to an end laura about a manly al jazeera

